          "content_id": null,
          "type": null
        },
        "content": "If $X_1\\sim N(\\mu_1, \\sigma_1^2)$ and $X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2, \\sigma_2^2)$ are *independent* random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, we have\n\n$$\naX_1+bX_2\\sim N(a\\mu_1+b\\mu_2, a^2\\sigma^2_1+b^2\\sigma^2_2).\n$$\n\nFor example, if $X_1\\sim N(2,3)$ and $X_2\\sim  N(1,4)$ are independent, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2} & ∼N(2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 1,{2}^{2}\\cdot 3+{3}^{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ∼N(4+3,4\\cdot 3+9\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ∼N(7,48),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{X}_{1}-3{X}_{2} & ∼N(2\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 1,{2}^{2}\\cdot 3+{3}^{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ∼N(4-3,4\\cdot 3+9\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ∼N(1,48).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "title": "Finding a Sum or Difference of Scaled Normal Random Variables"
      },
      "capture_key": "step-t5523",
      "observed_position": 7
    },
