      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W=-X+3Y & ∼N(-(-5)+3\\cdot 1,(-1)^{2}\\cdot 16+(3)^{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & ∼N(8,25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y∼N(8,25).$",
      "can_dont_know": false,
      "can_skip": false,
      "question": {
        "dom_id": "step-q109659",
        "name": "Question 10",
        "problem": "Suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(-5,16)$ and $Y∼N(1,1).$ Find the distribution of the random variable $W=-X+3Y.$",
        "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W=-X+3Y & ∼N(-(-5)+3\\cdot 1,(-1)^{2}\\cdot 16+(3)^{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & ∼N(8,25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y∼N(8,25).$",
        "calculator_instructions": "",
