      ],
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14275528/observations/step-q109674-769a1e5c29a237e81457.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14275528/observations/step-q109674-769a1e5c29a237e81457.png",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2} & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(7-8,5+4) \\\\  & ∼N(-1,9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-1$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{9}=3.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(-1.9\\le Y<-0.49).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.9\\le Y<0.49) & =P(\\frac{-1.9-(-1)}{3}\\le Z<\\frac{-0.49-(-1)}{3}) \\\\  & =P(-0.3\\le Z<0.17) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.17)-P(Z<-0.3) \\\\  & =Φ(0.17)-Φ(-0.3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.3) & ≈0.3821 \\\\ Φ(0.17) & ≈0.5675\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.9\\le Y<0.49) & =Φ(0.17)-Φ(-0.3) \\\\  & ≈0.5675-0.3821 \\\\  & ≈0.19.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "can_dont_know": false,
      "can_skip": false,
      "question": {
        "dom_id": "step-q109674",
        "name": "Question 15",
        "problem": "If ${X}_{1}∼N(7,5)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(8,4)$ are independent and $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2},$ calculate $P(-1.9\\le Y<-0.49).$ Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th width=\"25%\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$z$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3821\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3783\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3745\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4207\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4168\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4129\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4602\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4562\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4522\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th width=\"25%\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$z$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5596\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5636\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5675\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5987\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6026\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6064\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6368\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6406\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6443\\,$ </td></tr>\n\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n\n</tbody></table>",
        "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2} & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(7-8,5+4) \\\\  & ∼N(-1,9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-1$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{9}=3.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(-1.9\\le Y<-0.49).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.9\\le Y<0.49) & =P(\\frac{-1.9-(-1)}{3}\\le Z<\\frac{-0.49-(-1)}{3}) \\\\  & =P(-0.3\\le Z<0.17) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.17)-P(Z<-0.3) \\\\  & =Φ(0.17)-Φ(-0.3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.3) & ≈0.3821 \\\\ Φ(0.17) & ≈0.5675\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.9\\le Y<0.49) & =Φ(0.17)-Φ(-0.3) \\\\  & ≈0.5675-0.3821 \\\\  & ≈0.19.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "calculator_instructions": "",
