      "solution_binding": "bae8572c1527436f6d20278a7c08d6db1f7ada445d5a2ca63110723ee5121aa2",
      "accepted": true,
      "feedback": {
        "dom_id": "step-q109652",
        "name": "Question 7",
        "problem": "If ${X}_{1}∼N(-2,1)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(-3,10)$ are independent, then what is the distribution of $Y=3{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}?$",
        "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y=3{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2} & ∼N(3\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (-3),{3}^{2}\\cdot 1+{2}^{2}\\cdot 10) \\\\  & ∼N(-12,49).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y∼N(-12,49).$",
        "calculator_instructions": "",
