    {
      "key": "step-q109730",
      "question": {
        "dom_id": "step-q109730",
        "name": "Question 13",
        "problem": "If ${X}_{1}∼N(9,8)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(4,2)$ are independent and $Y={X}_{1}-2{X}_{2},$ calculate $P(Y\\le 4).$ Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ | $\\,0.7823\\,$ | $\\,0.7852\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ | $\\,0.8051\\,$ | $\\,0.8078\\,$ | $\\,0.8106\\,$ | $\\,0.8133\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ | $\\,0.8315\\,$ | $\\,0.8340\\,$ | $\\,0.8365\\,$ | $\\,0.8389\\,$ |",
        "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}-2{X}_{2} & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}-2{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+4{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(9-8,8+8) \\\\  & ∼N(1,16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =1$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{16}=4.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(Y\\le 4).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le 4) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{4-1}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.75) \\\\  & =Φ(0.75)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.75)≈0.7734.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le 4)≈0.77.\n$$",
        "calculator_instructions": "",
