      },
      "solution_binding": "521a96d1099861098f75f69a8f29f3b366bd923ae1c23d4edd11287b6baa987c",
      "accepted": true,
      "feedback": {
        "dom_id": "step-q109750",
        "name": "Question 17",
        "problem": "In a factory, cans are filled with soda. The amount of soda in one can is normally distributed with a mean of $340\\,\\text{g}$ and a variance of $4\\,{\\text{g}}^{2}.$ The weight of a can is normally distributed with a mean of $25\\,\\text{g}$ and a variance of $1\\,{\\text{g}}^{2}$ If one can containing soda is randomly selected, what is the probability that it weighs more than $360\\,\\text{g}?$ You may assume that the weights of soda and cans are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0102\\,$ | $\\,0.0099\\,$ | $\\,0.0096\\,$ | $\\,0.0094\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.0132\\,$ | $\\,0.0129\\,$ | $\\,0.0125\\,$ | $\\,0.0122\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.0170\\,$ | $\\,0.0166\\,$ | $\\,0.0162\\,$ | $\\,0.0158\\,$ |",
        "worked_solution": "Let $X$ denote the weight of the soda, and let $Y$ denote the weight of the can. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(X+Y>360).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(340,4)$ and $Y∼N(25,1)$, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X+Y & ∼N(340+25,4+1) \\\\  & ∼N(365,5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $X+Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =365$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nIn order to find $P(X+Y>360),$ we convert $X+Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>360) & =P(Z>\\frac{360-365}{\\sqrt{5}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-2.24) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le -2.24) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-2.24)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(-2.24)≈0.0125.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>360) & ≈1-Φ(-2.24) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0125 \\\\  & ≈0.9875.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the probability that a randomly selected can with soda weighs more than $360$ grams is $0.9875.$",
        "calculator_instructions": "",
        "fields": [
          {
            "key": "selection",
            "type": "radio",
            "choices": [
              {
                "option": "a",
                "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-109750-a",
