      "solution": {
        "status": "ambiguous",
        "reasoning": "The difference D=algebra−geometry is normal with mean 75−62=13 and variance 125+110=235. Thus P(D>0)=1-\\Phi(-13/\\sqrt{235}). Rounding the z-score to -0.85 and using the supplied table gives 1−0.1977=0.8023, choice a. This table approximation follows the example's method. No captured MA answer or grade establishes this question's key.",
        "confidence": 1,
        "answers": [
          {
            "key": "selection",
            "correct_value": "0.8023",
            "correct_option": "a",
            "value_type": "math",
            "wrong_value": null,
            "correct_keys": [],
            "wrong_keys": [],
            "ma_value": null,
            "ma_basis": "none",
            "ma_evidence": "",
            "disagrees": false
          }
        ]
      },
      "solution_binding": "864deb5c4db5cb67bbbfbe5a2256b56e7eef995fa28c00a32874893ff903eca1",
      "accepted": true,
      "feedback": {
        "dom_id": "step-q108976",
        "name": "Question 20",
        "problem": "The results of a group of students on an algebra test are normally distributed with a mean of $75$ and a variance of $125$, while the results of the same group on a geometry test are normally distributed with a mean of $62$ and a variance of $110.$ \nIf one student is randomly selected, what is the probability that they scored higher on the algebra test? You may assume that the student's scores on both tests are independent. \n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2005\\,$ | $\\,0.1977\\,$ | $\\,0.1949\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ | $\\,0.1894\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2296\\,$ | $\\,0.2266\\,$ | $\\,0.2236\\,$ | $\\,0.2206\\,$ | $\\,0.2177\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.2611\\,$ | $\\,0.2578\\,$ | $\\,0.2546\\,$ | $\\,0.2514\\,$ | $\\,0.2483\\,$ |",
        "worked_solution": "Let $X$ denote the student's algebra test result, and let $Y$ denote the student's geometry result. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(X>Y)=P(X-Y>0).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are  independent random variables such that $X∼N(75,125)$ and $Y∼N(62,110)$, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X-Y & ∼N(75-62,125+110) \\\\  & ∼N(13,235).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the variable $X-Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =13$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{235}.$\n\nIn order to find $P(X-Y>0),$ we convert $X-Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X-Y>0) & =P(Z>\\frac{0-13}{\\sqrt{235}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-0.85) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.85)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.85)≈0.1977.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X-Y>0) & ≈1-Φ(-0.85) \\\\  & ≈1-0.1977 \\\\  & =0.8023.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the probability that the student scored higher on the algebra test is $0.8023.$",
